<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Elchingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1495 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Elchingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Benedictine abbey above the Danube near Ulm whose abbots ruled a small territory, and which gave its name to Napoleon's battle of 1805.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The abbey was founded on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the early 12th century by local nobles related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> and settled by Benedictine monks. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-ulm">Free Imperial City of Ulm</a> held its advocacy for long periods, and the abbey achieved full imperial status at the end of the 15th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot governed several villages as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-circle">Swabian Circle</a> and of the Swabian bench of prelates. Disputes with Ulm, whose Protestant territory surrounded the abbey, were frequent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elchingen">Elchingen</a> remained Catholic and in the 18th century rebuilt its church in late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> and early neoclassical style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey was secularised in 1802 and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. On 14 October 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-ney">Marshal Ney</a> defeated an Austrian force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-elchingen">Battle of Elchingen</a> during the Ulm campaign and was later made Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elchingen">Elchingen</a>. The abbey church survives in Oberelchingen.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-elchingen.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Elchingen" width="298" height="473"><figcaption>Image: OwenBlacker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulm-Wiblingen-Fugger.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1495 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Elchingen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Elchingen Abbey</dd></dl></aside></div>
